# The Rise and Demise of Commercial Geography.

**Authors:** Handley, Lawrence R.

## Abstract
Commercial geography, originally taught in 18th-century trading schools, reached its zenith in the mid-1920s because it was stimulated by the development of the British Empire, noted for its commercial applications, and popularized through information disseminated by geographical societies. Demise factors include America's isolationist attitudes, development of specialized fields, and an unscientific approach. (RM)

